for (int n : numbers) {
    Fibonacci task = new Fibonacci(n);
    Thread t = new Thread(task);
    t.start();
}

Are those four numbers guaranteed to print in the order 8, 13, 21, 34?,"Yes - the loop starts the threads in that order, so they print in that order","No. Starting in order does not mean finishing in order

Real code:

// Main
int[] numbers = {6, 7, 8, 9};

for (int n : numbers) {
    Fibonacci task = new Fibonacci(n);
    Thread t = new Thread(task);
    t.start();
}

The loop never calls join(), so main reaches the end of its own code almost immediately. Do the four numbers still get printed?,No - when main ends the JVM stops and the threads are killed,"Yes. These are ordinary (non-daemon) threads, and the JVM keeps running until they all finish, so main ending early does not stop them",Only the first number is printed,"No, the program throws an exception when main ends with threads running",,2,"main is just one thread among several. The JVM shuts down once all ordinary threads are done, not when main happens to finish.",2

Real code:

public class MyTask implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(3000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("error...");
        }
    }
}

public class Waiter implements Runnable {

    private Thread threadToWaitFor;

    public Waiter(Thread threadToWaitFor) {
        this.threadToWaitFor = threadToWaitFor;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            System.out.println("start waiting ");
            threadToWaitFor.join();
            System.out.println("enough waiting");
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("error...");
        }
    }
}

// Main - demo 2
Thread t2 = new Thread(r1, "sleepy_thread");   // MyTask sleeps 3000 ms

Runnable r2 = new Waiter(t2);
Thread t3 = new Thread(r2, "waiter");

t2.start();
t3.start();

Thread.sleep(100);
System.out.println(t3.getState());

MyTask sleeps 3000 ms. Give the printed output of demo 2 and the rough timing of each line.,"start waiting immediately, then enough waiting immediately after","start waiting almost immediately, then a pause of about 3 seconds while t2 sleeps, then enough waiting once t2 has finished","enough waiting first, then start waiting",Only start waiting is printed - enough waiting never runs,,2,"The Waiter prints, then blocks inside t2.join() until t2's 3 second sleep is over, then prints again. The gap between the two lines IS the join.",2

Real code:

// Main - demo 3
Counter c1 = new Counter();

Runnable countRunner = new CounterRunnable(c1);   // both threads share ONE counter
Thread p1 = new Thread(countRunner, "person1");
Thread p2 = new Thread(countRunner, "person 2");

p1.start();

Thread.sleep(100);
p2.start();

System.out.println(p1.getState());
System.out.println(p2.getState());

p1.join();
p2.join();

System.out.println(c1.getCount());

In demo 3, which line of output can only be produced after BOTH threads have completely finished, and why?,"System.out.println(p1.getState()), because a state can only be read at the end","System.out.println(c1.getCount()), because it comes after p1.join() and p2.join(), which each block until that thread is done","System.out.println(p2.getState()), because p2 started last",None - all of the lines can print at any time,,2,The two join() calls are what make the final count trustworthy. Without them main could print the count while the threads were still incrementing.,2

Real code:

public class Sleeper implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(3000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("error");
        }
    }
}

// Main
for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    Sleeper task = new Sleeper();
    Thread t = new Thread(task);
    t.start();
}

Sleeper sleeps 3000 ms. Roughly how long from the start of the loop until all four threads have finished?,About 12 seconds - four sleeps of 3 seconds each,"About 3 seconds - all four threads sleep at the same time, because a sleeping thread does not need a core","About 3 seconds, but only on a machine with 4 or more cores","Unpredictable, anywhere between 3 and 12 seconds",,2,"Measured: all four finished in just over 3 seconds. Waiting overlaps for free, and it does not depend on core count at all.",2

Real code:

public class Sleeper implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(3000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("error");
        }
    }
}

// Main
for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    Sleeper task = new Sleeper();
    Thread t = new Thread(task);
    t.start();
    t.join();          // note the position
}

The only change is that join() is now INSIDE the loop. Roughly how long does this version take?,Still about 3 seconds,"About 12 seconds. join() inside the loop waits for each thread to finish before starting the next one, so the sleeps run one after another instead of overlapping",About 6 seconds,It deadlocks and never finishes,,2,"Measured: 12016 ms, versus 3002 ms with join() moved after the loop. A join() in the wrong place quietly removes every benefit of using threads at all.",2

Real code:

public class Sleeper implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(3000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("error");
        }
    }
}

// Main
Thread[] ts = new Thread[4];

for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    Sleeper task = new Sleeper();
    ts[i] = new Thread(task);
    ts[i].start();
}

for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    ts[i].join();
}

Two separate loops - start them all, then wait for them all. How long, and why is this the right shape?,"12 seconds, the same as joining inside one loop","About 3 seconds. Every thread is started before any waiting begins, so all four sleeps overlap and the second loop just collects them","About 3 seconds, but the count of finished threads will be wrong",It cannot compile - join() must directly follow start(),,2,Measured: 3002 ms. Start-all-then-join-all is the standard pattern precisely because it keeps the overlap while still guaranteeing everything is finished before you continue.,2

