One thread runs this MyTask. Roughly how long does that thread take, and where does nearly all the time go?,"About 3 seconds, almost entirely in Thread.sleep(3000) - the six println calls take a negligible amount of time","About 3 seconds, mostly spent in the for loop","About 15 seconds, because the loop runs 5 times with a sleep each time",A few milliseconds - sleep does not block the thread that calls it,,2,"The sleep is outside the loop, so it happens once. Printing six lines is microseconds against a 3 second wait.",1

Real code:

public class Counter {

    private int count = 0;

    // critical section
    public synchronized void increment() {
        count++;
        try {
            Thread.sleep(5000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("Interrupted");
        }
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

public class CounterRunnable implements Runnable {

    private final Counter counter;

    public CounterRunnable(Counter counter) {
        this.counter = counter;
    }

    public void doWork() {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 1000; i++) {
            counter.increment();
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        doWork();
    }
}

// Main - demo 3
Counter c1 = new Counter();

Runnable countRunner = new CounterRunnable(c1);   // both threads share ONE counter
Thread p1 = new Thread(countRunner, "person1");
Thread p2 = new Thread(countRunner, "person 2");

p1.start();

Thread.sleep(100);
p2.start();

System.out.println(p1.getState());
System.out.println(p2.getState());

p1.join();
p2.join();

System.out.println(c1.getCount());

increment() sleeps 5000 ms and is synchronized. Two threads each call it 1000 times. Roughly how long before the final count is printed?,About 5 seconds - the two threads sleep at the same time,"About 10,000 seconds, roughly 2.8 hours. synchronized allows only one thread inside increment() at a time, so all 2000 sleeps happen one after another instead of overlapping","About 5000 seconds, roughly 1.4 hours, since the two threads share the work",About 100 seconds,,2,"2000 calls times 5 seconds = 10,000 seconds. The lock is what makes the sleeps queue up. This is why the demo can show the states but never actually reaches the final count in a class.",2

