public class Counter {

    private int count = 0;

    // critical section
    public synchronized void increment() {
        count++;
        try {
            Thread.sleep(5000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("Interrupted");
        }
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

You want the same demo to finish quickly in class while still showing the same three states. Which changes achieve that? (select all that apply),Reduce Thread.sleep(5000) inside increment() to about 50 ms,Reduce the loop in CounterRunnable from 1000 iterations to about 10,Remove the synchronized keyword,Remove the sleep from increment() entirely,Increase the number of threads from 2 to 20,"1,2","Verified: 50 ms and 10 iterations gives the same TIMED_WAITING, WAITING and BLOCKED readings with a correct final count, in about a second. Removing synchronized breaks the count, and removing the sleep makes BLOCKED far too brief to observe.",2

Real code:

// Main - demo 1
Runnable r1 = new MyTask();          // MyTask sleeps 3000 ms
Thread t1 = new Thread(r1, "thread_1");

t1.start();

Thread.sleep(100);
System.out.println(t1.getState());

// Main - demo 2
Thread t2 = new Thread(r1, "sleepy_thread");   // MyTask sleeps 3000 ms

Runnable r2 = new Waiter(t2);
Thread t3 = new Thread(r2, "waiter");

t2.start();
t3.start();

Thread.sleep(100);
System.out.println(t3.getState());

Demos 1 and 2 run one after the other, and main sleeps 100 ms in each. Roughly how much time does main itself spend on these two demos?,"About 6 seconds, since each MyTask sleeps 3 seconds","About 200 ms. main only waits for its own two 100 ms sleeps - it never joins the worker threads, so it does not wait for their 3 second sleeps",About 3.2 seconds,Almost no time - Thread.sleep in main is ignored,,2,main's timing is driven purely by its own sleeps. The workers are still sleeping in the background when main moves on to demo 3.,2

Real code:

// Main - demo 1
Runnable r1 = new MyTask();          // MyTask sleeps 3000 ms
Thread t1 = new Thread(r1, "thread_1");

t1.start();

Thread.sleep(100);
System.out.println(t1.getState());

MyTask's sleep is changed from 3000 ms to 50 ms, with main's Thread.sleep(100) left alone. What does the printed state become?,Still TIMED_WAITING,TERMINATED - the task's 50 ms sleep is already over by the time main wakes up after 100 ms and reads the state,"NEW, because the thread finished before being observed",BLOCKED,,2,A good trap: the demo only works because main's wait is SHORTER than the task's. Whenever you read another thread's state you are really asking 'where had it got to when I looked'.,2

