            Thread.sleep(3000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("error...");
        }
    }
}

// Main - demo 1
Runnable r1 = new MyTask();          // MyTask sleeps 3000 ms
Thread t1 = new Thread(r1, "thread_1");

t1.start();

Thread.sleep(100);
System.out.println(t1.getState());

MyTask sleeps 3000 ms and main reads the state after only 100 ms. What is printed?,RUNNABLE,"TIMED_WAITING - the thread is inside Thread.sleep, which is a wait with a time limit",WAITING,BLOCKED,,2,"Verified output: TIMED_WAITING. Anything with a deadline - sleep(ms), join(ms), wait(ms) - lands in TIMED_WAITING rather than WAITING.",1

Real code:

public class Waiter implements Runnable {

    private Thread threadToWaitFor;

    public Waiter(Thread threadToWaitFor) {
        this.threadToWaitFor = threadToWaitFor;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            System.out.println("start waiting ");
            threadToWaitFor.join();
            System.out.println("enough waiting");
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("error...");
        }
    }
}

// Main - demo 2
Thread t2 = new Thread(r1, "sleepy_thread");   // MyTask sleeps 3000 ms

Runnable r2 = new Waiter(t2);
Thread t3 = new Thread(r2, "waiter");

t2.start();
t3.start();

Thread.sleep(100);
System.out.println(t3.getState());

The Waiter calls threadToWaitFor.join() with no timeout. What state does t3 (the waiter thread) report?,TIMED_WAITING,"WAITING - join() with no argument waits for as long as it takes, so there is no time limit and the state is the untimed one","BLOCKED, because it is waiting for another thread","RUNNABLE, because join() does not change a thread's state",,2,"Verified output: WAITING. This demo exists purely to separate WAITING from TIMED_WAITING - same idea of not running, and the only difference is whether a deadline exists.",2

